    <p>
      Bob Zybach's first working life was in reforestation. He was lead man at
      Pacific Reforestation, Inc. of Lake Oswego from 1966 to 1970, and
      president of his own firm, Phoenix Reforestation, Inc. of Eddyville,
      Oregon, from 1970 to 1991 — a company that <em>Inc.</em> magazine ranked
      332nd on its first Inc. 500 list of fastest-growing private companies in
      December 1982. From 1981 to 1985 he was vice-president, editor and then
      associate editor of the <em>A.R.C. Quarterly</em>, the Associated
      Reforestation Contractors' trade journal, which reached about 1,500
      readers nationally and concentrated on worker safety and on replanting
      federal and industrial forestland in the western states. Those dates
      overlap his return to Oregon State University: a certificate in Northwest
      Studies in January 1990 — one of the last two the program awarded — and a
      B.S. from the College of Forestry in June 1991, in forest resource
      recreation, with an academic focus on cultural resources management.
    </p>

    <!-- source: "Curriculum Vitae", nwmapsco.com/ZybachB/Curriculum_Vitae.htm, read
         4 September 2026 - 1989-1996 OSU Research Forests, Cultural Resources Management
         Forester; MAIS September 3, 1999 (Forest Ecology; Cultural Anthropology; Historical
