         website in existence". -->
    <p>
      From 1989 to 1996 he was cultural resources management forester for the
      OSU Research Forests, and that is the job this collection came out of.
      The Soap Creek Valley History Project, he wrote in the introduction he
      signed at Corvallis on 11 June 1990, "was undertaken by Oregon State
      University (OSU) Research Forests in 1989 for the purpose of better
      understanding the history and culture of an area impacted by OSU forestry
      and agricultural practices for nearly sixty years." He made the Soap
      Creek recordings himself; of the whole twenty-year run of tapes his
      thesis says that "only two people, Jackson (1978-1980 OSU College of
      Forestry recordings) and Zybach (1989-1997 Soap Creek Valley
      recordings), conducted almost all interviews." Fifteen monographs were
      printed between 1990 and 2009; he is named as an author on thirteen of
      them, led ten, and his own site lists him as the series editor. Then he
      wrote the series up. <em>Using Oral Histories to Document Changing Forest
      Cover Patterns: Soap Creek Valley, Oregon, 1500-1999</em> was presented on
      5 March 1999, ran to 321 pages, and earned a Master of Arts in
      Interdisciplinary Studies that September in forest ecology, cultural
      anthropology and historical archaeology. A Ph.D. in environmental
      sciences followed on 12 December 2003, for a dissertation titled
      <em>The Great Fires: Indian burning and catastrophic forest fire patterns
      of the Oregon Coast Range, 1491-1951</em>. He
      has been president of NW Maps Co. since 1994 and program manager of the
      Oregon Websites and Watersheds Project since 1996 — an organization
      conceived in the autumn of 1996 whose first website went online on 15
      January 1997 and which describes itself as "likely the oldest independent
      educational website in existence." He sat on the Benton County Historic
      Resources Commission from 1994 to 2002 as its historic resources
      protection chair. The work since has kept the same shape: cultural
      resource inventories of Alberta Street in Portland with high-school
      students in 1992 and again in 2015; <em>Coquelle Trails</em>, written
      with Coquille chief Don Ivy in 2013; and the 545-page <em>Jerry Phillips'
      Elliott State Forest Oral Histories</em>, published in 2019 — the second
      series in this collection. Three awards mark the range: the Oregon
      Heritage Excellence Award in 2009, accepted on behalf of ORWW and the
      Confederated Tribes of Grand Ronde; an Oregon Black Pioneers award in
      2019 for lifetime achievement in Oregon Black history research,
      particularly on Letitia Carson; and the Coos County Historical Society's
      Nathan Douthit Local Historian Award in 2023.
    </p>

    <h2 id="own-words">In his own words</h2>

    <p>
      Three passages from the 1999 thesis, printed here as they are printed
